{title2=Special Relativity}

= a
{parent=18f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Conservation of momentum in the parent's rest frame gives $p_A+p_B=0$. Equal daughter masses and the <relativistic energy-momentum relation> then give equal daughter energies. Energy conservation fixes
$$
\boxed{E_A=E_B=\frac{Mc^2}{2}.}
$$
Using $E^2=p^2c^2+m^2c^4$ and the specified direction of A,
$$
\boxed{p_A=-\frac c2\sqrt{M^2-4m^2},\qquad
p_B=+\frac c2\sqrt{M^2-4m^2}.}
$$
The velocities follow from $v=pc^2/E$:
$$
\boxed{v_A=-V,\quad v_B=V,\qquad V=c\sqrt{1-4(m/M)^2}.}
$$
These are spatial momenta in the original <inertial frame>. For positive daughter rest mass, $m<M/2$ gives $0<V<c$.

= b
{parent=18f}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Before its second decay A moves uniformly with $x=-Vt$. Its <Lorentz factor> is
$$
\gamma_A=\frac1{\sqrt{1-V^2/c^2}}=\frac{M}{2m}.
$$
The <proper time> along its <world line> satisfies $d\tau=dt/\gamma_A$. Since the first decay occurs at the spacetime origin, integration gives
$$
\boxed{t=\frac{M}{2m}\tau,\qquad x=-\frac{MV}{2m}\tau.}
$$
Equivalently these are the components of A's constant <four-velocity> times $\tau$. The assumption $m>0$ is needed for a daughter rest frame and its proper-time clock; a massless daughter has neither.
